 will be switching contacts now  to my presentation  so um as promised i wanted to talk about  how pi spark actually works so um for  those of you who don't know pi spark is  actually  a api  a spark api and what it allows you to do  is write spark applications using python  syntax or the python api  and pi spark supports most of sparks  features such as as you can see here sql  data frame streaming ml lib and the  spark core  but what  what this means and the reason for this  api is because spark is actually written  in scala um and its source code can be  compiled to java bytecode and it has to  be ran on the java virtual machine so  the main takeaway here is that pi spark  is actually not python native so when  you do an import of pi spark and you try  to execute you know pi spark um  apis you're actually relying on  python you know sending instructions to  to be ran on the jvm  um i've ran into like errors if you  don't install your jvm correctly and  things like that um so it could be a  little bit cumbersome when when um  working with pi spark but i overall it  is a very nice way to  kind of access you know spark features  through python and keep your um your  code base very python um syntax friendly  um and this might face a bit of a  problem for data science applications  because most of us like writing in  python right  most data science teams you know are  familiar with lots of python libraries  um and you you you would use like  scikit-learn and whatnot um and all of  those code all that code has to be  executed on you know python uh native  engine um but then when you're right in  pi spot you're actually executing on the  jvm so you actually um have to balance  you know right um have to balance kind  of  two types of logic running on in two  different kinds of environments  so what that means is is that you want  to maintain a balance of things that you  need to maintain and things that you  want  that you also want so  so in this case like i said on the right  you have your python logic where it has  to be ran on the python interpreter and  then you also have your spark logic  which needs to be run on the jvm and  it's good to also think about what kinds  of data that you need and want to  process so on the left you might have  pandas data frames in terms of like mock  data frames where you're you know  running very small unit tests in terms  of um  making sure that your python  functionality works um and then uh on  the  or in terms of like spark data frames  where you're running kind of like an  integration test to make sure that um  kind of your spark application works  end-to-end  so  that brings me to the types of tests  that you want to  write so like i said an integration test  that could be  very similar to kind of making sure that  your overall application runs when you  deploy it in a distributed fashion so  making sure that you know when you  ingest data from your  data lake and  running things in spark making sure that  that runs through together with your  python logic and your sparklogic  and unit tests and  unit tests which is you know writing  kind of small modular pieces of code to  make sure every individual function kind  of works so as to give your future kind  of data scientists and developers  confidence to contribute to your code  base and then the last kind of  test which is  more subtle  but very  important to your developer experience  is like type checks making sure that you  know you have the right kinds of data  types that are  the right kinds of data types that you  expect so that when you deploy your  spark applications in production you are  your code base is expecting this the  same kinds of data types that  you have a developed fit for  um  yeah so let's break that down so that's  kind of like all of the different things  that you want and you need so let's kind  of break that down into  this 2x2 table  in order to  kind of understand the different  scenarios of how you'd be processing  data and how that affects your  kind of execution time  so on the top left you have like a  pandas data frame and your python logic  and that's the easiest test because  there's no cluster spin up and on the  right hand side um you have your spark  data frame and your python logic you  might have to encounter a spark uh  your cluster spin up because your data  frames are already partitioned in a  spark data frame format you might have  to convert your spark data frame to  pandas which can be very expensive  depending on the size of your data  and in fact you might run into memory  issues so what some developers have done  very similar to my demo is either wrap  your python functionality into a pandas  udf or use the map in pandas api  and that way you can successfully extend  functionality of pi spark yet  be able to write  you know python functions and be able to  use python libraries  the only thing is that has to be  serializable  so it might be a bit cumbersome to test  on this type of functionality and it  also might be expensive  um and i'll go into a little bit more  about udfs later on and then on the  bottom left you have your pandas data  frame and your spark logic and this is  the case where maybe you might not think  you would run into this ever but um a  small i guess like a comment uh one  scenario could be that you have your  pandas mock data frames and you have  your kind of like spark logic that has  to be coupled with like some python  logic together with the spark lodging  and because of that you would still have  to encounter a cluster spin up because  spark logic has to run on the spark  cluster  and then  because of that you also have to convert  your pandas data frame to a smart data  frame and that might be cumbersome to  test as well because of that data  conversion  and then finally um you have the spark  and spark and in this case you're pretty  happy but you also have to encounter a  local spark cluster spin out  so  like i said udfs and pandas udf they  might kind of come into the rescue of  of saving you that data conversion  because instead of um  converting your spark data frames to  pandas what  spark actually has thought about is  serializing your python  function so that it is is able to run on  different worker nodes um and actually  has seen a lot of improvements and i  wanted to also talk a little bit about  the evolution of  being able to run your python  native  functionality on spark and how that has  improved over time  so the first kind of improvement is that  running your python logic will now  encounter less time so prior to spark  2.3 i know we're at spark 3.2 so this is  a little bit of like three or four three  years in history um so in spark 2.3  you have the improvement of the pandas  udf so what that means is that it um it  sparked work used um the apache aero  project in order to  help serialize some of these um  functions  or i guess like reserves the right to  determine how to partition your pandas  series  so  what that means is that in the past it  your pi like spark doesn't know what  your python function does right so in  order to be as  conservative as possible it actually  processes your data one row at a time so  that's what you see in the blue bars  here and  in spark 2.3 they came out with the  pandas udf  that actually allows them to  instead of um i guess processing things  one row at a time it recognizes that you  know things can be in a in a series in a  column column or format and it can kind  of partition it in a way where the  workers can run kind of chunks of data  and not just one row at a time so  what that means in terms of performance  is huge performance gains and as you can  see the largest performance gain in this  case is the cdf function which is a  cumulative distribution function  that is  taken from scipy  so in scipy  it is a python package so that's one of  the reasons why i wanted to talk about  this it's another really nice way of  ex